Subject: register VM: borrow `this`, a cached global and numeric fast-path results instead of dup+adopt

What this does:
  The register VM's deferred-RC ownership flip (0142) made a register store a
  borrow, and with it the frame and heap reads (get_local, get_arg,
  get_var_ref, the field and element reads) already store borrowed values.
  Two read opcodes and the arithmetic fast-path stores were left on the old
  owning form:

  - load_this dup'd `f->this_obj` (or the global object) and then adopted the
    dup straight back into the destination. Both owners outlive the
    instruction -- the frame owns this_obj, the runtime owns the global -- so
    the register can borrow. Only a non-strict primitive receiver, which has
    to be boxed and can allocate, still makes a new reference, and it stays in
    the slow handler.

  - get_global dup'd the value a filled global-cell cache hit found. The
    cell's object slot owns that value, so it borrows the same way. A new
    `js_gv_cell_peek` is `js_gv_cell_probe` without the dup; the stack VM's
    OP_get_var keeps the owning probe.

  - the arithmetic fast paths (T_FAST_RRR, T_FAST_UN, T_FAST_SHAPE,
    T_FAST_RRR_F64 and the five per-shape binary handlers, plus regvm_t_unary)
    stored with T_SET_NEXT_P, which adopts. `regvm_num_fast` and
    `regvm_unary_num_fast` return only immediates -- an int, a float or a
    boolean -- so there is no producer reference to give up and the adopt was
    a six-instruction tag test that never released anything. They now store
    with T_SET_NEXT_B. The generic cold halves keep T_SET_NEXT_P:
    `js_regvm_binary` can hand back an owned string or object.

  The monolith's load_this arm is changed to match (it is unreachable for this
  opcode under QJS_REGVM_THREAD, but a THREAD=0 build runs it).

  With deferred RC off the store macros dup as before, so both builds stay
  correct.

  Measured (`fixed.js`, 32 iterations, i7-8700): 24,558,165,446 ->
  24,139,931,233 instructions (**-0.418 G, -1.70 %**) and 11,479,956,505 ->
  11,411,074,466 cycles (noisy, ~-0.6 %), against the plan's 0.3 G expected /
  0.15 G floor for this item; the two read opcodes are ~0.402 G of that and
  the numeric stores ~0.016 G. Checksum `546.1008391043414` exact; diff-suite
  `pass=398 fail=0 skip=0` mono and threaded; test262 50/81192 errors (<= 50,
  baseline equal); ASan+UBSan box2d clean and a test262 subset error set equal
  to the reference; ASan with `zct_limit` forced to 1 (every safepoint
  reconciles) on box2d reports no use-after-free; leak assertion clean.
